Known behavioural deviations
----------------------------

 internaldict answers with a dictionary that holds nothing and has room for
 nothing, and that dictionary is read-only where PLRM 8 says the internal
 dictionary is writeable. PLRM 8 documents no member of it, says its contents
 change at any time, and says the operator is absent from some interpreters,
 so nothing here is a member a program was entitled to; the machinery's own
 dictionary is a separate object that the password does not open. The one use
 the specification sanctions is constructing a Type 1 font program, which
 reaches this dictionary while the array of procedures the font carries is
 being built: the standard array asks it for somewhere to keep the procedure
 a flex is drawn through, and reads its length against its capacity before
 writing. A dictionary with no room is an answer that question has, so such a
 program keeps that procedure and finishes building the array. What it cannot
 do is leave a procedure of its own here for a charstring to reach later,
 and no charstring would reach one: the glyphs are drawn through a face
 library that interprets the charstrings itself, draws flex and replaces stem
 hints without leaving that interpretation, and calls back into no
 PostScript, so a font's array of procedures is built here and never run.

 A font name carrying a nul is resolved as the shorter name it begins
 with. findfont hands the characters to the host's font configuration as
 C text, which ends at the first nul, so /Times-Roman with a nul after it
 answers with Times-Roman's face where PLRM 3.3 makes the two different
 names. The configuration cannot be asked the question any other way: the
 empty name is not a name it has not got and it may decline to answer at
 all, and the characters with the nul disguised match the same face back
 by resemblance. What a program is told correctly is availability --
 .fontnameavailable and /Font resourcestatus read the whole name and
 report such a name as unavailable.

 An operator that reads the characters of a string operand raises
 invalidaccess when the string's access withholds that read, and seven of
 them do so although their PLRM 8.2 entries do not list the error among
 the ones they raise: charpath, file, run, deletefile, renamefile,
 findfont and currentdevparams. The rule those seven are held to is not
 an operator's own -- PLRM 3.3.2 has an access attribute restrict the
 operations allowed on an object's value, and the invalidaccess entry
 names the read of an execute-only string among the violations it is
 raised for -- and it is a rule about what is done to the string rather
 than about which operator does it. charpath is defined as the outlines a
 show of the same string would make, and show's own entry lists the
 error; a filename or a font name is read exactly as status and
 setdevparams read theirs, and those entries list it too. Answering
 instead would leave each of the seven able to report what an unreadable
 string spells.

 What a text operator paints comes out of the font's Encoding,
 CharStrings and Metrics, so it reads them, and an access that withholds
 that read raises invalidaccess. charpath is the one of those operators
 whose PLRM 8.2 entry does not list the error. The rule it is held to is
 not its own -- PLRM 3.3.2 has an access attribute restrict the
 operations allowed on an object's value, and the invalidaccess entry
 names accessing a value in violation of its access among the causes it
 is raised for -- and charpath is defined as the outlines a show of the
 same string would make, whose entry does list the error. Answering
 instead would leave charpath able to report what a font's Encoding
 assigns to a character code that the program may not read.

 Idiom recognition does not match a template procedure, or a candidate
 procedure, whose access withholds the read the comparison needs, where
 PLRM 3.12.1 has the objects' attributes disregarded during that
 comparison just as eq disregards them. Comparing such a procedure means
 reading a value its access does not allow to be read (PLRM 3.3.2), and
 the comparison runs on every bind while idiom recognition is on:
 refusing there would let a single sealed template make every bind in a
 job fail, with an error bind's own PLRM 8.2 entry does not give it. What
 is lost is a substitution, which is an optimisation hook; the procedure
 bind returns is the one it bound.

