Implementation limits
---------------------

 xpost is built in two widths and its limits differ between them. In the
 narrow build, its primary, an array, string or dictionary holds up to 65,535
 elements (PLRM Appendix B.1); the wide build (WANT_LARGE_OBJECT) carries them
 to whatever memory holds instead. Past either, limitcheck.

 There is a second limit on composites that Appendix B does not name, and a
 program inside every limit that appendix does list can still reach it: how
 many composites may exist at once. Every string, array and dictionary takes
 one number from a table however large it is, and there are 524,287 of those
 numbers in the narrow build and 2,147,483,647 in the wide, of which the last
 thousand are not the program's: they are what the interpreter reports the
 exhaustion with.
 Past that, limitcheck -- vmstatus says nothing about this limit, and the
 "maximum" it reports is not this ceiling.

 A composite the program can no longer reach gives its number back when the
 collector next runs, so what the limit counts is composites held at once,
 not composites made. Not all of them are the program's own: a gsave and its
 grestore spend about four numbers between them, released like any other
 garbage, where a save and its restore spend none. How many numbers each of
 local and global VM has handed out is counted, but not through any route a
 program has: the operator reporting it is machinery, and a run is answered
 with it only when it was started to take a census of itself.

 A process holds up to 128 execution contexts at once, the same in both
 builds. PLRM leaves the count
 implementation-defined -- fork raises limitcheck at the ceiling -- and the
 Display PostScript Client Library manual puts a server's on the order of 50
 to 100. The table is allocated once with the interpreter, so an idle slot
 costs only its structure.

Interpreter parameters
----------------------

 PLRM Appendix C tabulates fifty-one parameters: seventeen user parameters
 in Table C.1 and thirty-four system parameters in Table C.2. This
 interpreter answers with twenty-two of them and adds four of its own.

 Carried, and reported by currentuserparams: MaxFontItem, MaxExecStack,
 MaxDictStack, MaxOpStack, VMThreshold, VMReclaim, IdiomRecognition,
 HalftoneMode, MaxFormItem. By
 currentsystemparams: MaxFontCache, CurFontCache, MaxDisplayList,
 CurDisplayList, GenericResourceDir, GenericResourcePathSep, RealFormat,
 ByteOrder, Revision, PrinterName, BuildTime, MaxFormCache,
 CurFormCache.

 [/] setsystemparams sets the one system parameter that has a setter, and
     that is all there is to set. MaxFontCache is the glyph cache's
     capacity, and a request for it goes to the same place setcacheparams'
     request goes -- refused outside a system administrator job, as PLRM
     8.2 requires -- so which door a program knocks at no longer decides
     whether anything happens.

     The rest are not settable because there is nothing behind them to
     set, rather than because the operator declines: RealFormat and
     ByteOrder describe the machine; the resource directory is the host's
     search path; the revision and the build stamp are the build's; the
     device is the one the run was started with; and PLRM Appendix G bars
     a page description from choosing how its marks are held, so the
     display list's bound and the spill policy are the invocation's. The
     Cur- figures are consumption and read-only by definition.

     currentsystemparams reads each figure back from the thing that owns
     it rather than from a second copy, so no two operators can report one
     parameter differently, and the build stamp is read through a call
     rather than frozen into the body -- a frozen one would be the stamp
     of whichever build wrote the virtual memory image.

 The absent twenty-seven, by the section that describes them. The other two
 the tables name are the passwords, which are dealt with below:

 [/] C.3.2  MinFontCompress. Of the font cache's three parameters the two
     that bound it are carried -- MaxFontItem, what may be cached, and
     MaxFontCache, how much cache there is. The third is not carried as a
     user parameter and currentuserparams does not report it, but neither
     is it quite absent: it is the middle operand of setcacheparams, which
     takes a value for it and records it nowhere, and currentcacheparams
     answers with one above MaxFontItem. Nothing here is compressed -- a
     cached glyph is held as the flat raster it was drawn into -- and
     PLRM C.3.2 makes a value above MaxFontItem the way to say exactly
     that, so the figure reported is true even though the parameter
     itself is not carried and cannot be moved.
 [ ] C.3.3  MaxUPathItem, MaxUPathCache, MaxPatternItem, MaxPatternCache.
     Two of the three caches these bound are not here at all. There is no
     user path cache and no pattern cache -- a pattern repaints its cell
     every tile, which is recorded above -- so for these four there is
     nothing to bound. The form cache is the third and is bounded:
     MaxFormCache and MaxFormItem are carried, and CurFormCache reports
     what its drawings come to.
 [ ] C.3.4  AccurateScreens, MaxScreenItem, MaxScreenStorage,
     MaxSuperScreen, MaxStoredScreenCache. A screen is built as asked and
     is not tuned by parameter.

     MaxScreenItem bounds what one screen may occupy, and here that is
     bounded already. The cell a screen tiles the plane with is chosen
     from a fixed table of lattice vectors, none longer than twenty-five,
     so a cell cannot hold more than about sixteen hundred values however
     the frequency, the angle or the device resolution are asked for.
     MEASURED: an ordinary screen at sixty lines per inch builds a cell of
     four by four -- sixteen values, some hundreds of bytes -- and no
     frequency from one to three hundred moved it. A parameter bounding
     that would be a limit on something that cannot grow, which is the
     kind this file exists to refuse rather than to add. HalftoneMode is the one of the group that
     is carried, and carried as the mode that is true: mode 0 has the
     halftone-setting operators behave as usual rather than put a product's
     own screen in place of the one asked for, which is what happens here.
     A request for either of the other modes is answered with 0, the rule
     the fixed stack sizes are answered by.
 [ ] C.3.5  MaxLocalVM. Local VM grows as a job needs it, bounded by the
     machine rather than by a parameter.
 [ ] C.3.6  FontResourceDir. It locates the files of the Font category,
     the one category the generic resource directory does not answer for.
     No font is found that way here: a face comes from the host's font
     configuration, so there is no directory of font resource files for
     this to be the name of.
 [ ] Table C.1  JobName, which names the current job for the status
     messages this interpreter does not display.
 [ ] Table C.2, identifying an installation this interpreter is not.
     LicenseID is an identifier Adobe assigns to a product, and there is
     none to report; PageCount counts the pages a device has printed
     since it was made, which wants a store that outlives a run and there
     is none; StartupMode governs a job run when a device is switched on,
     and nothing here is switched on. FactoryDefaults waits on
     setsystemparams reaching more than the one parameter it now sets.
 [ ] Table C.2, the source data a device holds beside its display list:
     MaxImageBuffer, MaxSourceList, CurSourceList, MaxDisplayAndSourceList.
     A source list holds an image's source data and the pixel arrays of
     uncached glyphs; nothing is held that way here -- image data goes
     through the filters to the raster as it is read -- so there is no
     store to bound and none to report. The last of the four is a ceiling
     over that store together with the display list, which is carried.
 [ ] Table C.2, the font outline cache, which the tables name and no
     section details: MaxOutlineCache, CurOutlineCache.
 [ ] Table C.2, the consumption figures for the caches above, which
     cannot be reported where the store they measure is not there to
     measure: CurPatternCache, CurUPathCache, CurStoredScreenCache.
 [X] Table C.2, CurScreenStorage, which reports one screen. PLRM C.3.3
     has it count the bytes all active halftone screens occupy, and
     C.3.4 says what an interpreter that builds and holds one screen at a
     time reports: the others "do not consume memory and are thus ignored
     when the current screen storage amount is calculated". A screen a
     gsave or a gstate saved is held here as the parameters it was set
     from and is built only on becoming current; a screen marks were made
     under is held inside the record of those marks, which CurDisplayList
     reports. The figure is the cell's own extent and not the length of a
     threshold array a program supplied, which may be longer.

 Setting a name that is not carried is accepted and has no effect, by
 either operator.

 [ ] ucachestatus, setucacheparams. PLRM C.3.3 gives these as the
     convenience operators for the user path cache, and 8.2 defines what
     they report. There is no such cache, and rather than say so the two
     answer for one: setucacheparams discards its request, and
     ucachestatus reports nought for every figure, which is a cache
     nothing can be kept in and is what is actually the case. What it
     does not do is refuse: PLRM has the operators answer, so they
     answer, and what they answer is the truth about a store that is not
     there.

 The two passwords are not among the absent: PLRM C.1.2 makes
 SystemParamsPassword and StartJobPassword write-only, settable by
 setsystemparams and not returned by currentsystemparams, and their absence
 from currentsystemparams is that rule rather than a gap. What they do here
 differs, and differs between them:

 [/] SystemParamsPassword. The password is kept and decides which kind of
     unencapsulated job startjob starts, which is what PLRM C.3.1 has it
     decide. What is not carried is a program's route to it:
     setsystemparams reaches neither password, so a job stream can satisfy
     one and cannot set or change one. The host sets it, beside the start
     job password, through the interpreter's own interface.
 [/] StartJobPassword. A password that is set is enforced whatever its
     value: PLRM C.3.1 has an empty one admit any job, and an empty one here
     admits only a job presenting an empty password. The rule as written
     would take away the lock a host gets by setting the password at all,
     which is the whole of what setting it is for.

 The two passwords are two tiers, and the difference between them is what a
 job may do to the jobs after it. An ordinary unencapsulated job, started
 with the start job password, may alter initial VM -- install a prolog,
 install a font -- and its work outlives it. A system administrator job,
 started with the system parameter password, may in addition change an
 implementation limit, which every job after it then runs under. The glyph
 cache's capacity is such a limit, and PLRM 8.2 allows setcacheparams to
 state one only in an administrator job for exactly that reason.

 What an unset password means depends on what the interpreter is being used
 as, because the safe answer differs. One run of one job has nobody to
 protect from the program it was started with, so with neither password set
 it admits at administrator level, which is the factory default C.3.1
 describes. A run reading a job stream serves jobs it did not choose, one
 after another, and admits only what it was configured to admit: an unset
 password there opens nothing, and with neither set no job in the stream
 becomes unencapsulated at all.

 In both, setting either password closes the tier beside it rather than
 leaving it open. A start job password with no system parameter password
 beside it admits ordinary jobs and no administrator, because a password
 that was set is a configuration someone made and a tier left unset beside
 it was left closed rather than left to fall back into.

 A password configured as the empty string is not the same as one never
 configured, and the difference is what the paragraph above turns on. The
 empty string is a password, presented by presenting nothing; never
 configured is no password and no tier to reach. So a job stream told
 nothing admits nobody, while one told that its password is empty admits
 whoever presents nothing -- which is a choice someone made rather than a
 default nobody looked at.

 The passwords are the host's. A program embedding the interpreter sets them
 through its interface, and not calling it leaves them unconfigured; a run
 that is not embedded takes them from XPOST_STARTJOB_PASSWORD and
 XPOST_SYSTEM_PARAMS_PASSWORD, where a variable that is absent is
 unconfigured and one set to nothing is the empty password. Neither is on
 the command line: an argument is readable by anything that can list
 processes.

 [/] One departure inside that, and it is the strict direction. C.3.1 has an
     empty system parameter password admit any job whatever password it
     presents. Here a start job password that is set is enforced whatever
     the other password is, because a host that locked the door with the
     start job password alone would otherwise find it open again, and a
     lock that a second unset password quietly removes is worse than no
     lock at all.

 RecordSpill, CurRecordSpill, MaxBandBytes and CurBandHeight are this
 interpreter's own rather than the language's, and doc/COMPAT records where
 they came from with the rest of the vocabulary that is not PLRM's.
 MaxBandBytes reports the same number as MaxDisplayList, because one figure
 answers two questions -- what a band of raster may cost, and what the
 record standing beside that band may cost. The specification names the
 second and not the first, Appendix G having declared the band device
 operators obsolete, so both names are kept rather than one being made to
 mean the other.
